\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Does a Vastly Skewed Income Distribution Affect Investment?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
September 17, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Economists at the University of Oxford and the University of California,
 Berkley, claim that the income disparity in the US between the top 1% earners
 and the rest of the country is the highest it has been since the 1920s.
 At this time, the bottom 40% of Americans barely retain any of the country's
 wealth, while just the top 1% has more of the country's wealth than 9 out
 of 10 Americans believe the top 20% should have.
 In another study, it has been suggested that the US experienced such expansive
 economic growth following WWII because the Pareto like distribution of
 income during that time was more evenly centered, unlike today with it
 being incredibly skewed to the right.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that the CDF of a Pareto random variable with parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{m}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F_{x}(x)=\mbox{Pr}(X\leq x)=\begin{cases}
1-\left(\frac{x_{m}}{x}\right)^{a} & \mbox{for}\ x\geq x_{m}\\
0 & \mbox{for}\ x<x_{m}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

and the PDF is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f_{x}(x)=\begin{cases}
a\frac{x_{m}^{a}}{x^{a+1}} & \mbox{for}\ x\geq x_{m}\\
0 & \mbox{for}\ x<x_{m}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

The Pareto type distribution is characterized by a scale parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{m}$
\end_inset

 and a shape parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

, which is known as the Pareto index.
 In my experiment, I hope to test the effect this distribution has on investment
 within a market.
 From expected utility theory, an agent becomes less averse to multiplicative
 risk as his wealth increases.
 Today, a good majority of the population has a reduced amount of wealth,
 which causes them to be more risk averse.
 This reduces the amount of money they invest.
 Even though the top 1% have had their wealth increase, the law of diminishing
 marginal utility will eventually take hold causing them to consume their
 wealth as opposed to investing it.
 Therefore, it is my belief that as the income distribution becomes more
 skewed, the total amount of investment will decrease.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, the two treatment variables are 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{m}$
\end_inset

, which will each take on three possible values: H(igh), M(edium), and L(ow).
 In the resulting 
\begin_inset Formula $3\times3$
\end_inset

 factorial design, each one of the nine treatments is employed in the same
 number 5 of trials.
 As a result, 45 trials are required to replicate the design 5 times.
 I will randomly assign the nine treatments in random order to the five
 trials in each replication.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A trial will be referred to as a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

session,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in which the same set of cohorts are employed.
 Every treatment consists of an identical setup: thirty cohorts are given
 an amount of wealth drawn from the Pareto distribution corresponding to
 the treatment.
 To reduce the payment amount, cohorts will be given lab francs, which they
 can convert to dollars at the end of a session.
 After wealth is distributed, all cohorts will be given the same randomly
 drawn price.
 Then, they will have the opportunity to invest their wealth in order to
 produce a common good, which can then be sold at the given price.
 The amount of the good that is produced will be random, with higher investments
 increasing the probability of obtaining a greater amount of the good.
\end_layout

